        <div id="what-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Is a Smart Thermostat? A Wi-Fi Thermostat That Runs the Schedule for You
          </h2>

          <p>
            A smart thermostat is a Wi-Fi connected thermostat that controls your furnace, air conditioner or heat pump the same way a standard one does, but adds an app, remote control, automatic schedules and, on better models, sensors that know when you are home. The heating and cooling equipment does not change. What changes is how often that equipment runs when nobody needs it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Programmable vs Smart</h3>

          <p>
            A programmable thermostat follows a schedule you punch in at the wall. A smart thermostat does the same job from your phone, and most also switch to an away setting on their own, using occupancy sensors or your phone's location (geofencing). The difference that saves money is that a smart thermostat keeps saving when your routine changes, while a programmable one keeps heating an empty house until someone reprograms it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Learning vs Scheduled</h3>

          <p>
            Learning thermostats, like the Google Nest Learning Thermostat, watch the changes you make and build a schedule from them. Scheduled smart thermostats, like the ecobee Essential and Google Nest Thermostat, follow a schedule you set in the app but still adjust automatically when everyone leaves. Learning is nice if you hate setup; scheduling is more predictable if your week is regular.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Smart Thermostats Save Money?</h3>

          <p>
            They can, mainly by cutting heating and cooling while you are out or asleep. Makers publish their own estimates: ecobee lists up to 26% for the Premium and Enhanced, Google up to 31% for the Nest Learning Thermostat, and Amazon cites the EPA estimate that Energy Star certified thermostats save an average of $50 a year. Treat those as maker figures. Real savings depend on your climate, your old habits and how far you let the temperature drift when you are away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compatibility" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Check Your System First: 24V, Line Voltage, Heat Pump and C-Wire
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best smart thermostat is the one that works with your wiring. Before comparing features, take a photo of the wires behind your current thermostat with the power off and answer four questions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is It a 24V System or Line Voltage?</h3>

          <p>
            Central furnaces, air conditioners, heat pumps and most boilers use low-voltage 24V control wiring: several thin wires with letters such as R, C, W, Y, G and O/B. Electric baseboard heaters and some wall heaters use line voltage, 120V or 240V, with two to four thick wires, often joined with wire nuts. A 24V smart thermostat cannot control line-voltage heat. Those systems need a line-voltage thermostat like the Mysa.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do You Have a C-Wire?</h3>

          <p>
            The C (common) wire gives a smart thermostat steady power for its screen and Wi-Fi. The Amazon Smart Thermostat and Honeywell Home X8S require one. The ecobee Premium includes a kit for homes without one, and Google says both Nest models work without a C-wire in most homes, with exceptions covered below.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat Pump, Furnace or Dual Fuel?</h3>

          <p>
            An O/B wire usually means a heat pump. Count the stages too: a W2 or Y2 terminal means a second stage of heat or cooling, and an AUX or E wire means backup heat. Check that stage count against each thermostat's listed limits before you buy.
          </p>

